Z||000014||Phil||07-09-99||04:02 PM||||The time involved in removing and replacing the stock exhaust manifolds is directly proportional to how many bolts you snap off. Time required to clean up the stock manifolds is about 2 hrs. each after they are welded.
Z||000015||Bruce||07-09-99||04:55 PM||||Can the bolt-breakage be prevented, or is it a fairly common problem with the Fiero V6? I would be taking it to an exhaust specialist, so any warnings I need to provide?
Z||000016||four50four||07-09-99||05:08 PM||||bolt breakage on manifolds of any fairly old vehicle is  very common
Z||000017||batboy||07-09-99||05:55 PM||||If you can get onto the exhaust bolts with a torch, heating them up first sometimes helps them come off easier and reduces breakage. Watch out for fuel lines and wires.
Z||000018||lowCG||07-09-99||06:46 PM||||I soak the nuts/bolts with a product called Maltby's,a penetrating solution that makes Liquid Wrench look like water, every day for about a week before attempting the manuever.<br>To tighten the nut/bolt a little bit before taking off works,and while taking off,go back a half a turn for every turn or so.This helps to disburse all the rust and grime that will come off and bind up the process,create galling,break things,etc.
